<<<JSON
{
  "summary_plain": "A working URL shortener you can run right now: POST a URL and get a short code, open the code and it redirects with a 302 while counting the click, and one page lists every link with its open count. It uses Node's built-in SQLite and HTTP server, so there are no runtime dependencies at all. The web page comes in two looks to choose from: a dark dense table ('tool') and a light card list with a form hero on top ('friendly'). Everything in the screenshots is real data from the real API, not mock-ups.",
  "screens": [],
  "demo": [
    {
      "command": "curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/links -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"url\":\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_with.html\",\"alias\":\"sqlite-cte\"}'",
      "output": "201 Created\n{\n  \"code\": \"sqlite-cte\",\n  \"url\": \"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_with.html\",\n  \"clicks\": 0,\n  \"shortUrl\": \"http://localhost:8080/sqlite-cte\"\n}"
    },
    {
      "command": "curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/links -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"url\":\"https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v26.x/api/sqlite.html\"}'",
      "output": "201 Created\n{\n  \"code\": \"KP4NLHy\",\n  \"url\": \"https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v26.x/api/sqlite.html\",\n  \"clicks\": 0,\n  \"shortUrl\": \"http://localhost:8080/KP4NLHy\"\n}\n\n(no alias given, so a random 7-character code)"
    },
    {
      "command": "curl -i localhost:8080/sqlite-cte",
      "output": "HTTP/1.1 302 Found\nlocation: https://www.sqlite.org/lang_with.html\ncache-control: no-store"
    },
    {
      "command": "curl -i localhost:8080/zzzzzzz",
      "output": "HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found\ncontent-type: text/plain\n\nNot found"
    },
    {
      "command": "curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/links -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{\"url\":\"ftp://nope\"}'",
      "output": "400 Bad Request\n{\n  \"error\": \"url must be a valid http(s) URL\"\n}"
    },
    {
      "command": "curl localhost:8080/api/links",
      "output": "200 OK\n[\n  {\n    \"code\": \"sqlite-cte\",\n    \"url\": \"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_with.html\",\n    \"clicks\": 24,\n    \"lastClickAt\": \"2026-11-18T11:30:18.741Z\",\n    \"shortUrl\": \"http://localhost:8080/sqlite-cte\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"code\": \"KP4NLHy\",\n    \"url\": \"https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v26.x/api/sqlite.html\",\n    \"clicks\": 4,\n    \"lastClickAt\": \"2026-11-18T11:30:18.720Z\",\n    \"shortUrl\": \"http://localhost:8080/KP4NLHy\"\n  }\n]"
    }
  ],
  "what_is_real": [
    "Shorten, redirect and stats all work end to end against a real SQLite file",
    "Short codes are 7 random characters from crypto.getRandomValues over a 56-char alphabet with look-alikes (0/O/1/l/I) removed, so links are not enumerable",
    "Every open is stored as its own row with a timestamp, so counts and 'last open' are computed, not stored counters",
    "URL validation (http/https only), alias rules (3-32 chars, reserved words blocked), 409 on a taken alias",
    "Both page designs are the real HTML/CSS, fed by the real /api/links endpoint",
    "Zero runtime dependencies: node:sqlite and node:http are built into Node 26"
  ],
  "what_is_faked": [
    "The five links in the screenshots were seeded by hand and the opens were generated with a curl loop",
    "Both design directions are served by the same app for comparison (/ and /_alt); the real build keeps only the one you pick and drops the other file and its route",
    "Copy-to-clipboard works in the browser but is not wired to any feedback beyond the button label",
    "No delete, no edit, no pagination on the link list; the real build should decide whether it needs them",
    "No tests were written, per the sketch rules; the brief asks for full test coverage and the real build must add it"
  ],
  "how_to_run": "npm install, then npm start, open http://localhost:8080 (the second design direction is at http://localhost:8080/_alt). Needs Node 24+ for the built-in SQLite; you are on 26.7.",
  "stack": ["Node.js 26.7 (node:http, node:sqlite — both built in)", "TypeScript 7.0.2 (type-check only, Node runs the .ts files directly)", "Plain HTML/CSS/JS page, no framework"],
  "verify_commands": ["npm run typecheck", "npm start"],
  "scope": {
    "size": "S",
    "risk": "low",
    "ui": true,
    "design_directions": true,
    "reason": "Small single-user app, three endpoints and one page, all of it already standing. The real work is mostly the test suite the brief asks for (unit tests for code generation and validation, integration tests for redirect and click counting), plus choosing one design, deciding the Node version floor for node:sqlite, and small hardening: 404 page instead of plain text, and handling the rare alias race."
  },
  "no_longer_shown": [],
  "directions": [
    {
      "id": "tool",
      "title": "Tool",
      "idea": "Dark and dense, built like a utility you leave open in a tab. Form is one row at the top, two big counters (links, opens) sit above a compact table with the code, target, opens, last open and a copy button. Shows the most links per screen and tells you when each was last used. On the phone the 'last open' column is dropped so the copy button still fits.",
      "screens": ["tool-1280.png", "tool-375.png"],
      "no_longer_shown": []
    },
    {
      "id": "friendly",
      "title": "Friendly",
      "idea": "Light, with a white hero at the top where shortening is clearly the main action, and the link list below as separate cards. Each card shows the code big, the target underneath, the open count with a small bar comparing it to your most-opened link. Easier on the eye and the bar makes popularity readable at a glance, but fewer links fit on screen.",
      "screens": ["friendly-1280.png", "friendly-375.png"],
      "no_longer_shown": ["The 'last open' time per link is not shown in this direction, only the total count"]
    }
  ],
  "nothing_to_show": false
}
